Reason:
The error check only logs a warning but does not return, so when `tps->partner` is an `ERR_PTR`, the subsequent call to `typec_partner_set_identity(tps->partner)` dereferences an invalid pointer, causing a crash.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
<text>
if (IS_ERR(tps->partner)) {
    dev_warn(tps->dev, "%s: failed to register partner\n", __func__);
    return;
}
</text>
Reason:
By adding an early `return` after the warning, execution stops when `tps->partner` is an error pointer, preventing the later dereference. The spelling of "partner" is also corrected.

